A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the maximum height reached by the particle, we use the kinematic equations for motion under constant acceleration. The initial velocity \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \), the final velocity at the maximum height \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), and the acceleration due to gravity \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (since it is downward).

Using the equation:
\[
v^2 = u^2 + 2as
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
0^2 = (20)^2 + 2(-9.8)s
\]
\[
0 = 400 - 19.6s
\]
\[
19.6s = 400
\]
\[
s = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.408 \, \text{m}
\]
