","The correct answer is \(\boxed{\text{Cu}^{2+} \text{ and } \text{Hg}^{2+}}\).  

**Explanation:**  
When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a solution of metal ions, sulfides are formed, and if the solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) of the sulfide is low, a precipitate forms. The sulfides of the metal ions in the mixture have different solubilities:  
1. **\( \text{Mn}^{2+} \):** \( \text{MnS} \) is soluble in acidic solutions due to the formation of soluble complexes like \( \text{Mn(H}_2\text{O)}_6^{3+} \).  
2. **\( \text{Ni}^{2+} \):** \( \text{NiS} \) is also soluble in acidic solutions.  
3. **\( \text{Cu}^{2+} \):** \( \text{CuS} \) is insoluble and precipitates as a black precipitate.  
4. **\( \text{Hg}^{2+} \):** \( \text{HgS} \) is insoluble and precipitates as a black precipitate.  

Thus, only \( \text{Cu}^{2+} \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions will precipitate as sulfides.  

