## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
Gary bought his first used car for $6,000. He borrowed the money from his dad who said he could pay him back the full amount over 5 years. Gary decided he would pay his dad back the full amount in a different number of years. Now, Gary is spending $150 more per month to pay the loan off in this new time frame instead of 5 years. How many years is the new repayment plan?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Let the initial cost of the car be $C = 6000$.
The original time frame is $T = 5$ years.
The annual cost is $6000$.
The monthly cost is $\frac{6000}{5 \times 12} = \frac{6000}{60} = 100$ dollars per month.

Let the new time frame be $t$ years.
The new monthly cost is $100 + 150 = 250$ dollars per month.
The total cost is $6000$, and the monthly cost is $250$.
The number of months in the new time frame is $t \times 12$.
The total cost is $250 \times 12 \times t = 6000$.

Solving for $t$:
$3000t = 6000$
$t = \frac{6000}{3000} = 2$

Thus, the new repayment plan is $\boxed{2}$ years.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

