\[
n = \frac{-1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 4a^2}}{2}
\]
For \( n \) to be an integer, \( 1 + 4a^2 \) must be a perfect square, i.e., \( 1 + 4a^2 = k^2 \) for some integer \( k \). Rearranging, we get \( k^2 - 4a^2 = 1 \), which simplifies to \( (k - 2a)(k + 2a) = 1 \). Since \( k \) and \( a \) are integers, the only possible solutions are \( k - 2a = 1 \) and \( k + 2a = 1 \), or \( k - 2a = -1 \) and \( k + 2a = -1 \).

1. \( k - 2a = 1 \) and \( k + 2a = 1 \): Adding the equations gives \( 2k = 2 \), so \( k = 1 \). Substituting back, we get \( 1 - 2a = 1 \), so \( a = 0 \), which is not a valid solution since \( a \geq 2 \).
