The lumped model is valid when the temperature gradient is negligible, meaning the temperature of the part is uniform.  

1. **Given Data:**  
   - Mass \( m = 1.2 \, \text{kg} \).  
   - Density \( \rho = 7150 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \).  
   - Surface area \( A = 0.075 \, \text{m}^2 \).  
   - Thermal conductivity \( k = 95 \, \text{W/m K/K} \).  
   - Specific heat \( c_p = 385 \, \text{J/kg K} \).  
   - Initial temperature \( T_0 = 345 \, \text{°C} = 368 \, \text{K} \).  
   - Ambient temperature \( T_{\infty} = 20 \, \text{°C} = 293 \, \text{K} \).  
   - Convective heat transfer coefficient \( h = 56.8 \, \text{W/m}^2 \text{K} \).  

2. **Calculate the Volume and Volume Volume Ratio (\( V/A \))**:  
   \[
   \text{Volume} = \frac{\text{Mass}}{\text{Density}} = \frac{1.2}{7150} = 1.68 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{m}^3.
   \]  
   \[
   \frac{V}{A} = \frac{1.68 \times 10^{-4}}{0.075} = 2.24 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{m}.
   \]  

3. **Calculate the Time Constant (\( \tau \))**:  
   \[
