","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton shift formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda \left( \frac{1 - \cos \theta}{2} \right)
\]

The maximum change occurs when the scattering angle \(\theta = 0^\circ\), which corresponds to the photon traveling directly back of the electron. In this case, \(\cos \theta = 1\), and the change in wavelength is zero. The maximum change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\), which corresponds to the photon traveling directly behind the electron. In this case, \(\cos \theta = -1\), and the change in wavelength is:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda \left( \frac{1 - (-1)}{2} \right) = \2\lambda
\]

The percentage change in the wavelength is:

\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times 100\% = \frac{2\lambda}{\lambda} \times 100\% = 200\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{200\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
