A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is calculated using Newton's second law:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m}
\]  
The force on the proton is given by the electric field:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]  
Substituting into the equation for acceleration:  
\[
a = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The time it takes for the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is calculated using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at
\]  
Since the proton starts from rest, \(v_0 = 0\).  
\[
35,000 \, \text{m/s} = 0 + (4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2) t
\]  
Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s}
\]  
Thus, the time it takes for the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(\boxed{7.25 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{s}}\).
