The EPR circuit is a quantum circuit that generates an maximally entangled state, known as the EPR state. The EPR circuit consists of two qubits and two quantum gates: a CNOT gate and a Hadamard gate. The EPR circuit can be represented as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{l}
\text{qubit 1: } |0\rangle \xrightarrow{\text{H}} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (|0\rangle + |1\rangle) \\
\text{qubit 2: } |0\rangle \xrightarrow{\text{CNOT}} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (|0\rangle + |1\rangle)
\end{array}
\]
The EPR circuit can be represented as a quantum circuit with 2 qubits. Thus, the quantum cost of the EPR circuit is 2.

The Tottowace function is a quantum function that can be represented as a quantum circuit with 3 qubits. The Tottowace function can be represented as follows:
\[
\text{Tottowace}(a, b, c) = (a + b) \cdot (a + c) \cdot (b + c)
\]
The quantum cost of the Tottowace function is 3.

