","To calculate the normal gravity \(g_n\) in mGal at a point on the terrestrial ellipsoid, we use the following formula:

\[
g_n = \frac{GM}{a^2} \left( \frac{1 + \frac{a}{b} \tan^2 \theta}{1 - \frac{a}{b} \tan^2 \theta} \right)^{1/2}
\]

where:
- \(G\) is the gravitational constant (\(6.6741 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2}\))
- \(M\) is the Earth's mass (\(5.9761 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg}\))
- \(a\) is the equatorial semi-axis of the ellipsoid (\(6356.241 \, \text{km}\))
- \(b\) is the polar semi-axis of the ellipsoid (\(6356.241 \times 0.9966 = 6290.461 \, \text{km}\))
- \(\theta\) is the latitude of the point (\(60^\circ\)S)

Substituting the given values:
\[
g_n = \frac{(6.6741 \times 10^{-8} \times 5.9761 \times 10^{24})}{6356.241^2} \left( \frac{1 + \frac{6356.241}{6290.461} \tan^2 60^\circ}{1 - \frac{6356.241}{6290.461} \tan^2 60^\circ} \right)^{1/2}
\]

First, compute the ratio \(a/b\):
\[
\frac{a}{b} = \frac{6356.241}{6290.461} = 1.012
\]

Next, compute \(\tan^2 60^\circ\):
\[
\tan 2\pi = \sqrt{3} \approx 1.732
\]

