This is derived from the second law of thermodynamics, which states that the entropy change \( \Delta S \) is given by:
\[
\Delta S = \frac{\Delta Q}{T}
\]
where \( \Delta Q \) is the heat transfer and \( T \) is the temperature.

In this case, the work done by the friction force is \( F_{\text{fr}} v \). This work is converted into heat energy, so \( \Delta Q = F_{\text{fr}} v \). Therefore, the entropy change is:
\[
\Delta S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
The entropy production rate is the rate of entropy change, so:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{\partial S}{\partial t} = \frac{\Delta S}{T} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
